{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mass.MirrorSplitIsASmallFractionOfTheEnergy","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mass.MirrorSplitIsASmallFractionOfTheEnergy","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Order.Ring.Defs","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Linarith"],"declarations":5,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.MirrorSplitIsASmallFractionOfTheEnergy.zero_neutron_flux_fraction_is_exactly_one_over_Z","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.MirrorSplitIsASmallFractionOfTheEnergy.zero_neutron_flux_fraction_is_exactly_one_over_Z","kind":"theorem","line":137,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/MirrorSplitIsASmallFractionOfTheEnergy.lean#L137-L146","doc":"**And the fraction is exactly `1/Z`, not merely bounded.**\n\nWith `Φn = 0` the energy is `c·Z·Φp` and the split is `c·Φp`, so `Z` times the\nsplit *is* the energy. On the `A = 2Z − 1` ladder that is `2/(A + 1)` — the same\n`1/A` order the general bound gives, reached exactly rather than as an estimate. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.MirrorSplitIsASmallFractionOfTheEnergy.zero_neutron_flux_is_the_r_eq_three_case","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.MirrorSplitIsASmallFractionOfTheEnergy.zero_neutron_flux_is_the_r_eq_three_case","kind":"theorem","line":121,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/MirrorSplitIsASmallFractionOfTheEnergy.lean#L121-L134","doc":"**The `Φn = 0` case satisfies the same bound at `r = 3`.**\n\nThe main bound needs `Φn > 0` to define a ratio, so a vanishing neutron flux\nformally escapes its hypotheses. It does not escape its conclusion: with `Φn = 0`\nand `N ≤ Z` (true on the whole `A = 2Z − 1` mirror ladder, where `N = Z − 1`), the\nsame inequality holds with `r = 3`.\n\nSo the limit that looked like an escape sits *inside* the excluded regime. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.MirrorSplitIsASmallFractionOfTheEnergy.interval_narrower_than_split_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.MirrorSplitIsASmallFractionOfTheEnergy.interval_narrower_than_split_iff","kind":"theorem","line":109,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/MirrorSplitIsASmallFractionOfTheEnergy.lean#L109-L119","doc":"**The obligation a two-sided bound would have to meet.** The interval is\nnarrower than the split it is meant to explain *exactly when* the combined slack\nis — and by `split_fraction_bound` the split is itself at most `(r − 1)/A` of the\nenergy. So the requirement is a joint relative tightness below `(r − 1)/A`.\n\nAn `iff`, not an implication: both directions are wanted, since the point is that\nnarrowing the interval is *equivalent to* tightening the bounds, with no slack\nelsewhere to trade against. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.MirrorSplitIsASmallFractionOfTheEnergy.interval_width","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.MirrorSplitIsASmallFractionOfTheEnergy.interval_width","kind":"theorem","line":103,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/MirrorSplitIsASmallFractionOfTheEnergy.lean#L103-L106","doc":"The interval `[B₁ − U₂, U₁ − B₂]` has width exactly the **sum of the two\nslacks**. Pure ring algebra; isolated so it is checkable on its own. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.MirrorSplitIsASmallFractionOfTheEnergy.split_fraction_bound","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.MirrorSplitIsASmallFractionOfTheEnergy.split_fraction_bound","kind":"theorem","line":80,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/MirrorSplitIsASmallFractionOfTheEnergy.lean#L80-L100","doc":"**The observable is a `1/A` sliver of the bounded quantity.**\n\nWith `Φn ≤ Φp ≤ r·Φn` and `Φn > 0`, the mirror split `Φp − Φn` scaled by the\nnucleon count `A = Z + N` is dominated by `(r − 1)` times the total\n`Z·Φp + N·Φn`. Read as a fraction: `ΔE / E ≤ (r − 1)/A`.\n\nStated multiplicatively so no division — and hence no field — is needed. ","signatureChunk":0}]}